{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at55441dee7f78c655be3fcd8dcfba37c1e5855a65/tree2330f3313fef14d95d373c8e258a7b01ed572ee0. Sole primary Source90f7d764 and full note read first against original1e8ef5d5 and immutableMainc40492ee.\n\nArchitecture:\n1. P2 — Apply class B's own test to the mixed/operational reads (sections2,3.1,3.6,D7). An at/head label alone does not make an answer reproducible at that position. sends/outbox includes mutable dispatcher attempts/acknowledgement (contract/read.ts81–100; reads.ts326–342). Operation status also includes driver bookkeeping (reads.ts345–359). Summary includes reservationExpiry from a fresh clock while returning the unchanged scope.head (reads.ts219–233), so both its reported time and expired IDs can change without an entry. MCP settle returns private custody phase/outcome/attempt/step data and can write a new custody outcome without a scope entry (service.ts819–875;custody.ts410–428). The internal row even calls jobState/readiness B while acknowledging no head; object.ts314–320/664–670 confirms the mismatch. Split recorded fields from advisory/custody/operational fields, classify those parts as D or explicitly mixed, and give them a disposition/limits. D7 currently covers only requests/incidents/waiting/status, leaving these surfaces outside the promised audit despite their B labels. Preserve read-only clock projection; this finding does not require adding journal entries for every advisory read.\n2. P2 — Separate the checker and token gateway's signed output from their own lifecycle (section3.4). The combined row labels both A, but Gateway.open/close/ended/forward are local APIs with GrantRecords.write, not signed intents judged in a scope (gateway.ts95–121,235–283,315–338). Checking token.state===live at266 does not turn that API or its stored forwarding count into an act. The checker similarly writes a run record before invoking a runner (checkers/service.ts13–18), while its submitted result acts are A. Give these local run/grant/custody surfaces a D disposition with their limits, or specify the declared native operation that records them. Keep the existing not-wired-in-production qualification. No new endpoint or blanket journalling is required.\n\nSecurity:\n3. P2 — Complete D5's authority and retry choices before calling it the retirement design. The current endpoint permits an admin OR an ordinary recorded controller to retire (hosted.ts90–103), and holds only the agent's signing seed. Native revoke-key requires membership.manage or the recovery key (membership.ts498–504/620–631); the default agent and ordinary controller do not have that action (roles145–162). A Control signature cannot be reused as a signature over a different native revoke-key intent. Name the signer and narrow authority path, including how controller-only retirement works. Do not solve it by giving every hosted agent membership.manage. State when retirement is considered complete and how a crash/lost revocation reply is recovered before irreversible seed deletion. Also choose the bearer replay rule: digest-only retained answers cannot reproduce the same plaintext bearer on the same op as today's hosted H1/control replay guarantees. Explicitly preserve that guarantee with defined private custody, or change it with a stated one-time/lost-reply result. These are required disposition choices, not requests to implement the full protocol in this document.\n4. P2 — State the actual read-session revocation bound (section3.6 and D21). The note says at most600seconds without its clock assumption. Issuance uses membership's clock, while acceptance checks the reading scope's clock; sessions.ts43–48 explicitly gives600seconds PLUS the difference between those clocks. No finite real-time revocation bound follows without a bound on that difference. State this limitation and distinguish key/member revocation, role changes at a new session, and immediate invalidation by replacing the deployment secret. The existing next-session statement alone hides the condition the security limit needs.\n\nSimplification: retain the four-class audit, named exceptions and proposed slices. Split mixed rows by the answer or lifecycle they actually expose; no additional general-purpose channel is needed. The source supports the central findings that credential take is private mutation, hosted retirement leaves the native key active, surface code shares the trusted document, and archive passes occur outside native operations. Device-command's current answer is a signed preparation with no outside operation, correctly distinguished from a real device effect.\n\nOther accuracy corrections while revising: the introduction says minting a clone token has no entry, but read-token and mint-read ARE recorded (as sections3.1/3.2 later state); name the one-time plaintext take instead. The wait row points to D13 (publication), while its disposition belongs with private configuration/custody. Publication's any-application/any-path rule is an explicit adopted owner decision in fold-publication section6, with activation/establishment as its trust boundary; present D13 as a proposed change to that policy, not evidence that the current code violates its approved rule. Its provider may also refuse a write. The document must not imply a demonstrated external exploit from path grammar alone.\n\nEvidence/validation: census of docs/mcp-tools.json confirms73=57dotted act names+16generic names; CLI dispatch, native route groups, view/bridge source, custody/control, token/site adapters, operations/import, device/simulator/presence, archive driver, observability and referenced future designs inspected as DATA. No project module/test/compiler/runtime executed. Exactly1newdocumentation path, no source/test changes; immutable git diff --check0. Per docs/testing.md no gate repeated for documentation only. Baseline treeef2c2b54e24f470551d360b7bcc50221410dd9cb; candidate tree2330f3313fef14d95d373c8e258a7b01ed572ee0. No source edits, network, merge, push or deployment by checker.\n\nFrozen checker DATA /private/tmp/checker-one-channel-draft1-v7LhWlmk, manifest271B0444 SHA3ab3ddf769574b660ae853437ad1b43e8081fae2270c42bbf499b54d13d23cea. Never execute/import/edit/chmod.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:90f7d764c431049eb097106448a4e45a79e6cbc9","binding":"assigned","head":"55441dee7f78c655be3fcd8dcfba37c1e5855a65","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0417d32f2b6829a19cbfa9226cde44a7565a761b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a5f0a7a54877b9ff616b66352ebfe25f1966ab11\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2a26c9287afb50c03b102b53d2ce564ee876ccf8","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}